 <tr><td><tt>PreorderArrayTree</tt></td><td>the nodes in a vector in
     preorder, with their parents</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ and \f$ O( n ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>BinaryTree</tt></td><td>pointers to the parent and to the two
     children</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ (the left and
     the right child)</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>CursorBinaryTree</tt></td><td>the same links as indices in a
     vector</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized</td><td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ArrayBinaryTree</tt></td><td>a positional vector, the children
     of \f$ i \f$ in \f$ 2 i + 1 \f$ and \f$ 2 i + 2 \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>linear in the subtree
     copied</td><td>\f$ O( 2^h ) \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 The links to the first child and to the next sibling give every ordered
 tree, whatever the degrees of its nodes, three pointers per node, and
 they make it a binary tree: the left child of a node is its first child and
 the right child its next sibling (figure 5.9 of the book, `to_binary()` of
 `BinaryTree.h`), a correspondence one to one between the ordered forests
 and the binary trees. The vector of parents (section 5.4) needs one
 integer per node and answers the parent alone, which is what the trees of
 the visits of a graph and the forests of the MFSET need; in preorder, the
 same vector knows the children too, the subtree of a node being the
 stretch of places that starts with it and ends at the first place whose
 parent precedes it. A complete \f$ d \f$-ary tree needs no link at all:
 numbered by levels from 0, the children of the node \f$ i \f$ are
 \f[
   d i + 1 , \ldots , d i + d , \qquad \text{its parent}
   \ \lfloor ( i - 1 ) / d \rfloor , \tag{1}
 \f]
 which is how a heap is stored (see \ref theory_priority); a
 binary tree far from complete wastes the places of the missing nodes, up
 to \f$ 2^{h+1} - 1 \f$ places for \f$ n = h + 1 \f$ nodes. The cursors put
 the nodes in one vector, so that the tree can be copied, saved or moved as
 a whole, and the indices stay valid when the vector grows.

 \section theory_tree_visits Visits

 A <em>visit</em> examines each node of a tree once: the
 <em>previsit</em> examines a node before its subtrees, the
 <em>postvisit</em> after them, the <em>invisit</em> with index \f$ i \f$
 after its first \f$ i \f$ subtrees (on a binary tree, with \f$ i = 1 \f$,
 the <em>symmetric</em> visit), and the visit <em>by levels</em> examines
 the nodes by increasing depth, by a queue (section 5.3).

 <em>Lemma 1 (the cost of a visit).</em> Each visit of a tree of \f$ n \f$
 nodes costs \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ time on the representations with the links
 to the first child and to the next sibling, or to the two children, and
 \f$ O( h ) \f$ space for its stack, explicit or of the calls.

 Indeed, each node is examined once, and each link is followed once from
 the node it leaves: the first child of a node when the node is entered, the
 next sibling when its subtree is over. The stack holds the nodes of the
 path from the root to the current node, i.e., at most \f$ h + 1 \f$ of
 them; the headers keep it explicit, since a tree of height \f$ n - 1 \f$
 would overflow the stack of the calls.

 The visits need no stack at all when the nodes have the link to their
 parent: the next node in symmetric order of a binary tree is the leftmost
 node of the right subtree, if there is one, and otherwise the lowest
 ancestor reached from a left child (`inorder()` of `BinaryTree.h`, and
 `PathIterator` of `Iterators.h`, which keeps the path instead of the links
 to the parents). A whole visit costs \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ even so, since
 each edge is walked once down and once up, while a single step costs
 \f$ O( h ) \f$. The <em>threads</em> of Perlis and Thornton reach the same
 end with no link to the parent: a missing left child is replaced by a link
 to the predecessor and a missing right child by a link to the successor,
 a bit of each node telling a child from a thread, so that the successor is
 the right thread or the leftmost node of the right subtree (`ThreadedTree`).
 The \f$ n + 1 \f$ links that a binary tree of \f$ n \f$ nodes leaves nil
 are exactly the threads.

 \section theory_tree_problems Problems on binary trees

 The functions of `BinaryTreeAlgorithms.h` work on any representation of
 the nodes with the links to the children, and to the parent where they need
 it. Two nodes \f$ u \f$ and \f$ v \f$ have their lowest common ancestor at
 the point where the paths from them to the root meet; once the deeper node
 has climbed to the depth of the other, the two climb together and meet
 there, i.e., \f$ O( h ) \f$ time and \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ space. A binary tree
 is a search tree if and only if each key lies in the open interval that its
 ancestors leave to it, which a previsit carrying the two ends checks in
 \f$ O( n ) \f$, while comparing each node with its children only is not
 enough. A search for the keys in \f$ [ a , b ] \f$ examines, beside the
 \f$ k \f$ keys reported, only the nodes on the two paths that search for
 \f$ a \f$ and for \f$ b \f$, i.e., \f$ O( h + k ) \f$ nodes. A sorted
 vector gives the search tree of least height,
 \f$ \lceil \log_2( n + 1 ) \rceil - 1 \f$, with the middle key at the root
 and the two halves below, in \f$ O( n ) \f$ by
 \f$ T( n ) = 2 T( n / 2 ) + O( 1 ) \f$.

 The previsit and the invisit of a binary tree of distinct values determine
 it, since the first value of the previsit is the root and splits the
 invisit into the two subtrees; a stack of the nodes whose right subtree is
 still open rebuilds it in \f$ O( n ) \f$, each node pushed and popped once.
 The <em>Cartesian tree</em> of a vector \f$ A \f$ (Vuillemin) has the
 leftmost minimum of \f$ A \f$ at its root and the Cartesian trees of the
 parts before and after it as its subtrees: it is the only binary tree whose
 invisit is \f$ A \f$ and which is a heap, and a stack of its right spine
 builds it in \f$ O( n ) \f$, since each item is pushed and popped once; the
 lowest common ancestor of two places in it is the minimum of \f$ A \f$
 between them, which ties the two problems together (Harel and Tarjan, and
 the range minimum of `RangeMinimum.h`).

 A heap of \f$ n \f$ items is built either by inserting the items one at a
 time, each climbing to its place (Williams, `HeapByInsertion.h`), or by
 sifting down the internal nodes from the last one to the root (Floyd). The
 insertions cost \f$ \Theta( n \log n ) \f$ in the worst case, an increasing
 vector, where each item climbs to the root, while the bottom-up
 construction costs \f$ O( n ) \f$: the \f$ \lceil n / 2^{l+1} \rceil \f$
 nodes of height \f$ l \f$ go down at most \f$ l \f$ levels each, and
 \f$ \sum_l l \, n / 2^{l+1} \le n \f$. On a random permutation the
 insertions cost \f$ O( n ) \f$ on average as well (Hayward and McDiarmid),
 and they are the method to use when the heap must be valid after each
 item.

 \section theory_tree_search Binary search trees

 A <em>binary search tree</em> holds a set of keys in the nodes of a binary
 tree so that, for every node \f$ u \f$, the keys of the left subtree of
 \f$ u \f$ are smaller than the key of \f$ u \f$ and those of its right
 subtree larger (section 6.1 of the book); hence the visit in symmetric
 order gives the keys in increasing order, and a search, an insertion and a
 removal go down one path from the root, in \f$ O( h ) \f$ time for a tree
 of height \f$ h \f$. The removal of a node with two children puts in its
 place its successor, the smallest key of its right subtree, which has no
 left child and is removed in its turn. Inserted in increasing order,
 \f$ n \f$ keys make a path, \f$ h = n - 1 \f$; inserted in a random order,
 the expected depth of a node is \f$ 2 \ln n + O( 1 ) \f$, i.e., about
 \f$ 1.39 \log_2 n \f$, by the analysis of the quicksort, whose pivots are
 the roots of the subtrees. A <em>balanced</em> tree keeps
 \f$ h = O( \log n ) \f$ whatever the order of the operations, by
 <em>rotations</em>: the right rotation of a node \f$ x \f$ with left child
 \f$ y \f$ makes \f$ y \f$ the root of the subtree and \f$ x \f$ its right
 child, the right subtree of \f$ y \f$ becoming the left one of
 \f$ x \f$, which keeps the order of the keys and changes the depths of the
 subtrees by \f$ \pm 1 \f$, in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ (figure 6.4 of the book).

 <table>
 <caption>The binary search trees of the area and the cost of their
          operations</caption>
 <tr><th>Tree</th><th>Balance</th><th>Height</th>
     <th>Search, insertion, removal</th><th>Rotations per update</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>BinarySearchTree</tt></td><td>none</td>
     <td>\f$ n - 1 \f$ in the worst case</td><td>\f$ O( h ) \f$</td>
     <td>none</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>RedBlackTree</tt></td><td>colors, (V1)-(V4)</td>
     <td>\f$ \le 2 \log_2( n + 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \log n ) \f$</td><td>at most 3</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>AVLTree</tt></td><td>heights of the children differing by at
     most 1</td><td>\f$ < 1.4405 \log_2( n + 2 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \log n ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( \log n ) \f$ on a removal, at
     most 2 on an insertion</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>Treap</tt></td><td>a heap of random priorities</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \log n ) \f$ expected</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \log n ) \f$ expected</td><td>fewer than 2
     expected</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>SplayTree</tt></td><td>none, each access splayed to the
     root</td><td>\f$ n - 1 \f$ in the worst case</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \log n ) \f$ amortized</td><td>\f$ O( \log n ) \f$
     amortized</td></tr>
 </table>

 A Red-Black tree colors its nodes red or black, with (V1) the root black,
 (V2) the leaves black, (V3) the children of a red node black, and (V4) the
 same number of black nodes on all the paths from a node to the leaves
 below it, its black height \f$ b( u ) \f$. By induction on the height, the
 subtree of \f$ u \f$ has at least \f$ 2^{b( u )} - 1 \f$ internal nodes
 (Theorem 6.1 of the book), and by (V3) at least half the nodes of a path
 from the root are black; hence \f$ n \ge 2^{h / 2} - 1 \f$, i.e.,
 \f$ h \le 2 \log_2( n + 1 ) \f$ (Theorem 6.3). An insertion colors the new
 node red, which may break (V3) only; the fault climbs by recolorings,
 two levels at a time, and is solved by at most two rotations. A removal of
 a black node breaks (V4) on one side, which a black token climbing by
 recolorings, and solved by at most three rotations, restores.

 <em>Lemma 2 (the height of an AVL tree, Adelson-Velsky and Landis).</em>
 An AVL tree of height \f$ h \f$ has at least \f$ F_{h+3} - 1 \f$ nodes,
 \f$ F_k \f$ the \f$ k \f$-th number of Fibonacci; hence
 \f[
   h < \log_\varphi\bigl( \sqrt{5} ( n + 2 ) \bigr) - 3 <
   1.4405 \log_2( n + 2 ) , \tag{2}
 \f]
 \f$ \varphi = ( 1 + \sqrt{5} ) / 2 \f$ the golden ratio.

 Indeed, let \f$ N_h \f$ be the fewest nodes of an AVL tree of height
 \f$ h \f$: the root of such a tree has a subtree of height \f$ h - 1 \f$, and
 the other, at least \f$ h - 2 \f$ high by the balance, is as small as
 possible; hence \f$ N_h = N_{h-1} + N_{h-2} + 1 \f$ with \f$ N_0 = 1 \f$ and
 \f$ N_1 = 2 \f$, i.e., \f$ N_h + 1 = F_{h+3} \f$; since
 \f$ F_k > \varphi^k / \sqrt{5} - 1 \f$ (\f$ F_k \f$ is the integer nearest to
 \f$ \varphi^k / \sqrt{5} \f$), \f$ n + 1 \ge F_{h+3} \f$ gives
 \f$ \varphi^{h+3} < \sqrt{5} ( n + 2 ) \f$, i.e., (2). An insertion changes by
 1 the heights on its path, and the lowest node left unbalanced, with a
 difference of 2, is rebalanced by a single rotation, if the higher grandchild
 is on the outside, or by a double one, which restores the height that the
 subtree had before the insertion, so that nothing above changes; a removal may
 shorten the subtree it rebalances, and the rotations may go on up to the root.

 A <em>treap</em> (Seidel and Aragon) gives each key a random priority and
 keeps the tree a binary search tree on the keys and a heap on the
 priorities, the root of each subtree of the highest priority in it. Its
 shape is then unique, that of the binary search tree built by inserting
 the keys by decreasing priority, i.e., in a random order, whatever the
 order of the operations: the expected depth of a node is
 \f$ O( \log n ) \f$, as for a random insertion order. A new key is
 inserted at a leaf and rotated up while its priority beats its parent's,
 and each rotation adds one node to the right spine of its left subtree or
 to the left spine of its right subtree; the rotations are therefore the
 lengths of those two spines at the end, whose expectation is less than 2
 (Seidel and Aragon), and a removal makes the same rotations in
 reverse.

 A <em>splay tree</em> (Sleator and Tarjan) keeps no balance at all: each
 access moves the node accessed to the root by rotations in pairs, a
 zig-zig when the node and its parent are both left (or right) children,
 two rotations from the top, and a zig-zag otherwise; the top-down splay
 does the same in one pass from the root, setting aside the keys met into a
 left and a right tree.

 <em>Lemma 3 (the access lemma, Sleator and Tarjan).</em> Let each node
 \f$ x \f$ have a weight \f$ w( x ) > 0 \f$, \f$ s( x ) \f$ the total weight
 of its subtree and \f$ r( x ) = \log_2 s( x ) \f$ its rank, and let the
 potential be \f$ \Phi = \sum_x r( x ) \f$. The splay of a node \f$ x \f$ in
 a tree of root \f$ t \f$ costs, amortized,
 \f[
   1 + 3 ( r( t ) - r( x ) ) = O\Bigl( \log \frac{ s( t ) }{ w( x ) } \Bigr)
   . \tag{3}
 \f]

 Indeed, a zig-zig or a zig-zag step that lifts \f$ x \f$ by two levels
 changes \f$ \Phi \f$ only on the three nodes it rotates, and the concavity
 of the logarithm bounds its two rotations plus that change by
 \f$ 3 ( r'( x ) - r( x ) ) \f$, \f$ r' \f$ the rank after the step; the
 last single rotation, if any, adds 1 more. The terms telescope along the
 path to \f$ 3 ( r( t ) - r( x ) ) + 1 \f$. With the weights all 1,
 \f$ r( t ) = \log_2 n \f$ and (3) is \f$ O( \log n ) \f$; since
 \f$ \Phi \le n \log_2 n \f$ at the start and \f$ \Phi \ge 0 \f$, a sequence
 of \f$ m \f$ operations costs \f$ O( ( m + n ) \log n ) \f$. With the
 weights the frequencies of the accesses, (3) gives the static optimality,
 i.e., a cost within a constant factor of the best static tree for those
 frequencies, with no knowledge of them.

 \section theory_tree_join Join-based algorithms

 The balanced trees above restore their balance inside each insertion and
 each removal. Blelloch, Ferizovic and Sun showed that one operation
 suffices instead, the <em>join</em>: \f$ \mathrm{join}( L , k , R ) \f$,
 for two balanced trees \f$ L \f$ and \f$ R \f$ whose keys are smaller and
 larger than \f$ k \f$, returns a balanced tree of all the keys, and every
 other operation (split, insertion, removal, union, intersection,
 difference, batches, filters) is written on join alone, once for all the
 schemes (`JoinAlgorithms.h`). The cost of join is bounded through a
 <em>rank</em> \f$ r( T ) = \Theta( \log n ) \f$: the height for the AVL
 trees, the black height for the Red-Black trees, \f$ \log_2 \f$ of the
 weight (the keys plus 1) for the trees balanced by weight, and the height
 in expectation for the treaps.

 <em>Lemma 4 (the join).</em> For each of the four schemes,
 \f$ \mathrm{join}( L , k , R ) \f$ costs \f$ O( | r( L ) - r( R ) | + 1 ) \f$
 (in expectation for the treaps), and the rank of its result is between
 \f$ \max( r( L ) , r( R ) ) \f$ and that plus 1.

 Indeed, if \f$ L \f$ is higher, the join goes down the right spine of
 \f$ L \f$ until it meets a subtree \f$ c \f$ of the rank of \f$ R \f$,
 makes the node \f$ ( c , k , R ) \f$ there, and fixes the balance on the
 way back by at most a double rotation per level. For the AVL trees the
 subtree met has height \f$ h( R ) \f$ or \f$ h( R ) + 1 \f$, the new node
 at most \f$ h( R ) + 2 \f$, and a single or a double rotation at the
 parent brings the difference back to 1; for the Red-Black trees the
 subtree met is black, of the black height of \f$ R \f$, the new node is
 red, and the only fault, a red node with a red child, climbs by one
 rotation per level and is solved at the root by painting it black; for
 the trees balanced by weight, with the parameter
 \f$ \alpha \le 1 - 1 / \sqrt{2} \f$, each level needs a single or a double
 rotation; for the treaps, join merges the two spines by the priorities,
 and \f$ k \f$ goes down until its priority beats those below, as an
 insertion does. The levels of the spine walked are
 \f$ | r( L ) - r( R ) | + O( 1 ) \f$.

 A split of \f$ T \f$ at \f$ k \f$ exposes the root \f$ ( L , m , R ) \f$,
 recurs on the side of \f$ k \f$, and joins the other side with \f$ m \f$
 and the part of the recursion on its side. The trees joined at the
 successive levels of the path have ranks that grow by the ranks of the
 subtrees left behind, so that by Lemma 4 the costs of the joins
 telescope to \f$ O( r( T ) ) = O( \log n ) \f$; `join2`, the join with no
 key in between, takes the largest key of \f$ L \f$ off it and joins it
 with \f$ R \f$, again in \f$ O( \log n ) \f$, and the insertion and the
 removal are a descent followed by joins on the way back.

 <em>Lemma 5 (the work of the set operations, Blelloch, Ferizovic and
 Sun).</em> The union, the intersection and the difference of two trees of
 sizes \f$ m \le n \f$, by expose, split and join, take the work
 \f[
   W( m , n ) = \Theta\Bigl( m \log\Bigl( \frac{n}{m} + 1 \Bigr) \Bigr) ,
   \tag{4}
 \f]
 which is optimal among the algorithms by comparisons, and, with the two
 recursive calls in parallel, the span \f$ O( \log m \log n ) \f$.

 The union exposes the root \f$ ( L , k , R ) \f$ of the smaller tree,
 splits the larger at \f$ k \f$, takes the unions of the two left and of
 the two right sides, and joins them with \f$ k \f$; the intersection and
 the difference differ only in whether \f$ k \f$ survives, and use `join2`
 when it does not. At the level \f$ i \f$ of the recursion the smaller tree
 has been cut into at most \f$ 2^i \f$ pieces, and the larger into at most
 \f$ 2^i \f$ pieces of sizes \f$ n_j \f$ summing to \f$ n \f$; their splits
 cost \f$ \sum_j \log( n_j + 1 ) \le 2^i \log( n / 2^i + 1 ) \f$ by the
 concavity of the logarithm. Summed over the \f$ \log_2 m \f$ levels, and
 with the joins costing no more than the splits (their rank differences
 telescope as in the split), this gives (4), which matches the lower bound
 \f$ \log_2 \binom{m + n}{m} \f$ of the comparisons needed to merge two
 sorted sequences (Hwang and Lin; Brown and Tarjan): it is
 \f$ \Theta( m + n ) \f$ for \f$ m \approx n \f$ and \f$ \Theta( \log n ) \f$
 for \f$ m = 1 \f$. The two recursive calls touch disjoint trees, and each
 level waits for one split and one join, whence the span. A sorted batch of
 \f$ m \f$ pairs is inserted (or removed) as the union (or the difference)
 with the tree that the batch would make, without building it, the batch
 being split by a binary search on the key of the root, in the same work;
 a filter and a map-reduce visit every node, in \f$ O( n ) \f$ work.

 \subsection theory_tree_augmented Augmented maps

 A node may keep, besides its size, a value of its subtree computed from
 those of its children and of its own pair, e.g., the sum or the maximum of
 the values; since every node that changes is rebuilt by
 \f$ \mathrm{node}( L , k , R ) \f$, which recomputes it, and a rotation is
 two such rebuilds, join keeps it, and so does every operation above
 (`JoinTree.h`). An <em>augmented map</em> (Sun, Ferizovic and Blelloch)
 takes a monoid \f$ ( A , \otimes , e ) \f$ and a map \f$ g \f$ of a pair
 into \f$ A \f$, and each node \f$ u \f$ keeps
 \f[
   \mathit{aug}( u ) = \mathit{aug}( u.\mathit{left} ) \otimes g( u.k , u.v )
   \otimes \mathit{aug}( u.\mathit{right} ) , \tag{5}
 \f]
 the combination of the values of its pairs in the order of the keys,
 which needs no commutativity. The augmented value of the keys in a range
 \f$ [ \mathit{lo} , \mathit{hi} ] \f$ is the combination of
 \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ pieces: from the node where the paths to
 \f$ \mathit{lo} \f$ and to \f$ \mathit{hi} \f$ part, the pairs of the path
 to \f$ \mathit{lo} \f$ not below \f$ \mathit{lo} \f$ with the right
 subtrees they leave behind, the pair of the node, and symmetrically on the
 path to \f$ \mathit{hi} \f$, at most two pieces per level of a balanced
 tree; the interval trees and the range trees of the library are augmented
 maps of this kind (see \ref theory_geometry_ranges).

 \section theory_tree_btree B-trees

 A <em>B-tree</em> of minimum degree \f$ t \ge 2 \f$ (Bayer and McCreight)
 is a search tree whose nodes hold several keys in increasing order: a node
 of \f$ j \f$ keys \f$ k_0 < \cdots < k_{j-1} \f$ that is not a leaf has
 \f$ j + 1 \f$ children, the child \f$ c_i \f$ holding the keys between
 \f$ k_{i-1} \f$ and \f$ k_i \f$, and the tree keeps the invariants
 \f[
   t - 1 \le j \le 2 t - 1 \text{ (the root: } 1 \le j \le 2 t - 1 \text{)} ,
   \qquad \text{all the leaves at the same depth} . \tag{6}
 \f]
 The keys of a node are read in one transfer from the secondary memory, a
 node filling a block, so that the measure of the cost is the number of the
 nodes read, the I/Os of the model of the two-level memory (Aggarwal and
 Vitter), rather than the comparisons, which a node of many keys answers in
 the main memory.

 <em>Lemma 6 (the height of a B-tree).</em> A B-tree of minimum degree
 \f$ t \f$ with \f$ n \ge 1 \f$ keys has height
 \f[
   h \le \log_t \frac{ n + 1 }{ 2 } , \tag{7}
 \f]
 and a search reads \f$ h + 1 = O( \log_t n ) \f$ nodes, with
 \f$ O( t \log_t n ) \f$ comparisons in all.

 Indeed, by (6) the root has at least 2 children, and every other node that
 is not a leaf at least \f$ t \f$; hence there are at least \f$ 2 t^{d-1} \f$
 nodes at the depth \f$ d \ge 1 \f$, each with at least \f$ t - 1 \f$ keys,
 and
 \f[
   n \ge 1 + ( t - 1 ) \sum_{d=1}^{h} 2 t^{d-1} = 2 t^h - 1 ,
 \f]
 which gives (7). The search goes down one path, from the root to a leaf at
 most, and in each node it finds the place of the key among at most
 \f$ 2 t - 1 \f$ keys. With \f$ t \f$ of the order of the keys of a block,
 e.g., \f$ t = 128 \f$, three levels index millions of keys, and the root and
 the level below it stay in the main memory.

 The insertion and the removal keep (6) with two operations on a node and its
 parent. A <em>split</em> of a full node \f$ y \f$ of \f$ 2 t - 1 \f$ keys,
 the child \f$ c_i \f$ of \f$ x \f$, moves its median key into \f$ x \f$ at
 the place \f$ i \f$ and its last \f$ t - 1 \f$ keys, with their children, into
 a new node, the child \f$ c_{i+1} \f$ of \f$ x \f$: both halves have
 \f$ t - 1 \f$ keys, and \f$ x \f$ one key more. A <em>merge</em> is its
 converse: two siblings of \f$ t - 1 \f$ keys each and the key of their
 parent between them become one node of \f$ 2 t - 1 \f$ keys; a
 <em>borrowing</em> moves the key of the parent down into a node and a key
 of a sibling with at least \f$ t \f$ keys up in its place, which keeps the
 order of the keys.

 <em>Lemma 7 (the operations in one pass).</em> `insert()` and `remove()` of
 `BTree.h` keep (6), each in one pass from the root down, with
 \f$ O( \log_t n ) \f$ splits, merges or borrowings of \f$ O( t ) \f$ each,
 i.e., \f$ O( t \log_t n ) \f$ time and \f$ O( \log_t n ) \f$ I/Os.

 Indeed, the insertion splits every full node it is about to enter, so that
 the parent of a full node always has room for the median; a full root
 splits under a new root, which is the only way the height grows, and keeps
 all the leaves at the same depth. The key goes into the leaf reached, which
 is not full. The removal enters only nodes with at least \f$ t \f$ keys
 (the root apart): before going down into a child of \f$ t - 1 \f$ keys it
 borrows a key from a sibling of \f$ t \f$ keys or more, or merges it with a
 sibling of \f$ t - 1 \f$, so that a key can always leave the node entered
 without breaking (6). A key found in an internal node is replaced by its
 predecessor, or by its successor, which lie in a leaf below a child with
 at least \f$ t \f$ keys, and is then removed from that leaf; if both
 children have \f$ t - 1 \f$ keys, they are merged around it, and it goes
 down with them. A root left with no key gives way to its only child, which
 is the only way the height falls.

 The B+-tree of section 6.3 of the book (`BPlusTree.h`) keeps the values in
 the leaves alone, the internal nodes holding the bounds of their children,
 i.e., the largest key below each child but the last, so that an internal
 node of a block holds more children and the tree is lower; the leaves are
 linked in a list in the order of the keys, and the \f$ k \f$ pairs of an
 interval are read in \f$ O( \log n + k ) \f$ time, by a search of its
 smallest key and a walk along the leaves, \f$ O( \log_t n + k / t ) \f$ I/Os
 when the leaves fill blocks. `BTreeMap.h` is a B+-tree in the main memory,
 where a node is a few lines of cache rather than a block: the place of a
 key is counted on all the places of a node at once, by comparisons of
 vectors on padded nodes, the lines of the next node are prefetched on the
 way down (Chen, Gibbons and Mowry), and the splits on the edges of the tree
 keep the old node full when the keys arrive in order, so that a sorted
 sequence fills all the nodes; with the counts of the pairs below each
 child it answers `rank()` and `select()` along one path, as the
 order-statistic trees do.

 <table>
 <caption>The B-trees of the area and the cost of their operations, for
          \f$ n \f$ keys, \f$ t \f$ the minimum degree, \f$ m \f$ the order
          and \f$ k \f$ the pairs of an interval</caption>
 <tr><th>Header</th><th>Values</th><th>Search, insertion</th>
     <th>Removal</th><th>Interval</th><th>Memory</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>BTree.h</tt></td><td>in every node</td>
     <td>\f$ O( t \log_t n ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( t \log_t n ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( t \log_t n + k ) \f$, by the iterator</td>
     <td>secondary, a node a block</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>BPlusTree.h</tt></td><td>in the leaves, linked</td>
     <td>\f$ O( m \log_m n ) \f$</td><td>none, as in the book</td>
     <td>\f$ O( m \log_m n + k ) \f$</td>
     <td>secondary, a node a page</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>BTreeMap.h</tt></td><td>in the leaves, linked</td>
     <td>\f$ O( ( L + I ) \log n ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( ( L + I ) \log n ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( ( L + I ) \log n + k ) \f$</td>
     <td>main, a node a few lines of cache</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_tree_dynamic Dynamic trees

 Beyond the book, the link-cut trees of Sleator and Tarjan (`LinkCutTree.h`)
 keep a forest of rooted trees that changes by the insertion of an edge
 between two trees (`link()`) and by the removal of an edge (`cut()`), and
 answer on it whether two nodes are in the same tree, the root of a tree,
 the lowest common ancestor of two nodes and the aggregate of the values on
 the path between two nodes, all in \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ amortized time; a
 forest kept as a vector of parents answers the same in time proportional
 to the depth, and one kept as the jumps of the binary lifting answers in
 \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ but must be built again, in \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$, at
 each change of its shape. Each node marks at most one of its children as
 <em>preferred</em>, so that the forest is split into <em>preferred
 paths</em>; each path is kept by a splay tree whose order is that of the
 depths, its leftmost node the shallowest, and the root of that splay tree
 points to the parent of the top of the path (a <em>path-parent</em>
 pointer, which no child pointer matches). The operation
 \f$ \mathrm{access}( v ) \f$ splays \f$ v \f$ in its splay tree, drops the
 deeper part of its path, and then climbs: at each path-parent \f$ x \f$ it
 splays \f$ x \f$ and makes the path below it the one just built, until the
 path from the root of the tree to \f$ v \f$ is a single splay tree with
 \f$ v \f$ at its root. The other operations are one or two accesses and a
 few pointers: `evert( v )` reverses the path from the root to \f$ v \f$
 after its access, by a flag pushed down lazily, which makes \f$ v \f$ the
 root; `link( u , v )` everts \f$ u \f$ and hangs it under \f$ v \f$;
 `cut( u , v )` everts \f$ u \f$ and accesses \f$ v \f$, which leaves
 \f$ u \f$ alone as the left child of \f$ v \f$; the aggregate of the path
 between \f$ u \f$ and \f$ v \f$ is the one kept at the root after
 `evert( u )` and `access( v )`, which is why the operation of the
 aggregates must be commutative.

 <em>Lemma 8 (the cost of an access, Sleator and Tarjan).</em> With the
 weight of each node the number of its descendants in the forest plus 1,
 i.e., with the ranks of (3) on the virtual tree made of the splay trees
 and of the path-parent pointers, and \f$ k \f$ the number of preferred
 paths that \f$ \mathrm{access}( v ) \f$ enters, the access costs, amortized,
 \f[
   O( \log n ) + k , \qquad \text{and} \qquad
   \sum k = O( ( m + n ) \log n ) \tag{8}
 \f]
 over any sequence of \f$ m \f$ operations.

 Indeed, the splays of an access telescope by (3) along the virtual tree,
 since the rank of the root of a splay tree is at most that of its
 path-parent, to \f$ 3 \log_2 n + O( k ) \f$; the \f$ k \f$ changes of the
 preferred child are bounded by the heavy-light argument, i.e., a change
 that makes a light child preferred happens at most \f$ \log_2 n \f$ times
 per access (a light child has at most half the descendants of its
 parent), and one that makes a heavy child preferred is paid by an earlier
 change of the other kind or by a link or a cut, each of which turns at
 most \f$ \log_2 n \f$ edges from heavy to light. The extra rotations of
 the \f$ k \f$ climbs are absorbed by doubling the constant of (3); hence
 every operation costs \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ amortized, and a sequence of
 \f$ m \f$ of them \f$ O( ( m + n ) \log n ) \f$.

 The weights of the edges are kept on nodes of their own, one in the middle
 of each edge, so that the maximum on the path between \f$ u \f$ and
 \f$ v \f$ is that of the values of the nodes; this is how the minimum
 spanning forest of `Reoptimization.h` finds, in \f$ O( \log n ) \f$
 amortized time, the heaviest edge of the cycle that a new edge closes,
 and swaps the two (see \ref theory_graph_dynamic). The experiments of
 Tarjan and Werneck rank the link-cut trees first among the dynamic trees
 when the operations are mostly links, cuts and queries on paths, the
 topology trees and the top trees being preferable for queries on subtrees.

